Polly is doing the right thing with expect to the repro (that unconditionally always throws) - it retries until the timeout is up, and then because the handler is failing, it throws the last failure observed (the `ApplicationException` in your case).

The `TimeoutRejectedException` will only be thrown if the user code (`foo` in your repro) causes cancellation - your handler doesn't do this, it just consistently fails until Polly gives up and tells you its failing.

In your original code (not the repro) I suggest you pass the `CancellationToken` to whatever asynchronous work you're performing, or if you can't pass it on for whatever reason (e.g. it's just your code doing a long loop or something) then your code should periodically call `ThrowIfCancellationRequested()` (e.g. at the start of each `for` loop iteration) so that it cooperatively responds to a request to cancellation, either from Polly's timeout strategy, or from any token passed to the strategy invocation itself.

This is essentially [what is called out in the documentation](https://www.pollydocs.org/strategies/timeout.html):

> **Important**
> It is crucial that the user's callback respects the cancellation token. If it does not, the callback will continue executing even after a cancellation request, thereby ignoring the cancellation.

The repro is ignoring cancellation, and thus you only see the errors.

