Hey folks, just couple of my comments since I worked on this directly and supporting the `Retry-After` header was in our minds from the beginning. 

Essentially, the `Polly.Core` supports this scenario by exposing the `DelayGenerator`. The generator can decide to override the delay provided by the Polly or just do no-op by returning `null`. By default,  `DelayGenerator` is null.

For HTTP scenarios, `Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience` exposes `HttpRetryStrategyOptions` where the `DelayGenerator` is initialized by default and handles `Retry-After` header. You even have a property to toggle this behavior on and off:

https://github.com/dotnet/extensions/blob/5fc05f874f74374749b01e284321168c7b92476a/src/Libraries/Microsoft.Extensions.Http.Resilience/Polly/HttpRetryStrategyOptions.cs#L46

