In Polly we are only reporting events after rejection by rate-limiter happens. What you want is to actively monitor the rate limiter used by the Polly. This can be archived by the following pseudo-code:

``` csharp 
var meter = new Meter("my.limiter");

using var registry = new ResiliencePipelineRegistry<string>();

registry.GetOrAddPipeline("rate-limiter-pipeline", (Action<ResiliencePipelineBuilder, ConfigureBuilderContext<string>>)((builder, context) =>
{
    var cancellation = new CancellationTokenSource();
    var limiter = new ConcurrencyLimiter(new ConcurrencyLimiterOptions
    {
        PermitLimit = 100,
        QueueLimit = 100
    });

    builder.AddRateLimiter(limiter);

    _ = MonitorRateLimiterAsync(limiter, cancellation.Token);

    context.OnPipelineDisposed(() =>
    {
        cancellation.Cancel();
        limiter.Dispose();
    });
}));

static async Task MonitorRateLimiterAsync(ConcurrencyLimiter limiter, CancellationToken cancellationToken)
{
    while (!cancellationToken.IsCancellationRequested)
    {
        var statisitcs = limiter.GetStatistics();

        // report metrics here

        await Task.Delay(1000, cancellationToken);
    }
}
```

Example above:

- Registers `rate-limiter-pipeline` with a rate limiter strategy.
- Creates a rate limiter strategy manually and registers active monitoring of it by pushing metrics to custom `my-limiter`.
- Register a dispose callback to properly cleanup the background task and manual rate limiter.

Ideally, `System.RateLimiting` APIs would produce native telemetry, however, that is a feature request to https://github.com/dotnet/runtime.

I don't think we need to do add anything new to Polly at this stage. If there are similar requests to this we might update our docs.

