All of the Controllers are marked as `internal`, so we can't create a new instance of them. That's why there are no methods or overloads where you could provide a controller during the policy setup.

Let me share with you two workarounds:
### Using a `Policy<object>`

You can create a single policy which can return anything (or nothing). 
With this approach you are loosing the strongly typediness but you can share the state. 

It is kinda the same idea what you can do for async methods with  `TaskCompletionSource<TResult>`:
```cs
TaskCompletionSource<object> tcs = new();
...
tcs.TrySetResult(null); //or simply calling the SetResult
``` 

### Using `Isolate` and `Reset` calls
Since Polly v4 we have two publicly exposed methods to [control manually the CB's state](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/Circuit-Breaker#manual-control--polly-v40): `Isolate` and `Reset`.

If you have two CB policies then you can use the `onBreak` and `onReset` delegates to control the other CB's state
```cs
ICircuitBreakerPolicy<StateA> policyA = Policy<StateA>
    .Handle<Exception>()
    .CircuitBreaker(2, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5));

var policyB = Policy<StateB>
    .Handle<Exception>()
    .CircuitBreaker(2, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5),
    onBreak: (_, __) => policyA.Isolate(),
    onReset: policyA.Reset);
```
The problem with this approach that you can't refer directly `policyB` from `policyA`, because it is defined later. 
But you can do that indirectly via `Context` object.
```cs
public static class ContextExtensions
{
    private static readonly string key = "OtherCB";
    public static Context WithCB<T>(this Context context, ICircuitBreakerPolicy<T> cb)
    {
        context[key] = cb;
        return context;
    }

    public static ICircuitBreakerPolicy<T>? GetCB<T>(this Context context)
    {
        return context.TryGetValue(key, out object logger) ? logger as ICircuitBreakerPolicy<T> : null;
    }
}
```
```cs
var policyA = Policy<StateA>
    .Handle<Exception>()
    .CircuitBreaker(2, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5),
    onBreak: (_, __, ctx) => ctx.GetCB<StateB>()?.Isolate(),
    onReset: (ctx) => ctx.GetCB<StateB>()?.Reset());

var policyB = Policy<StateB>
    .Handle<Exception>()
    .CircuitBreaker(2, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(5),
    onBreak: (_, __, ctx) => ctx.GetCB<StateA>()?.Isolate(),
    onReset: (ctx) => ctx.GetCB<StateA>()?.Reset());
```
The above code works only for two CBs but it can be easily extended to work with any number of CBs.

