If you don't have any place to meaningfully provide a `CancellationToken` value to the get request of the `HttpClient`, then an optimistic policy isn't really of much use to you if your overall HTTP timeout is of the order of a minute.

If the intention is to call it from a HTTP request (your code example is calling it like a sort of console app), then you could use [`HttpContext.RequestAborted`](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/microsoft.aspnetcore.http.httpcontext.requestaborted?view=aspnetcore-6.0), which can be automatically provided to MVC actions and Minimal API endpoints automatically by having a `CancellationToken` parameter on the method. This would automatically cancel the request if the client disconnected.

Otherwise the only way to provide a meaningful `CancellationToken` value through to your `HttpClient` is to create one yourself via a source such as [`CancellationTokenSource.Token`](https://docs.microsoft.com/en-us/dotnet/api/system.threading.cancellationtokensource.token?view=net-6.0).

```csharp
var cts = new CancellationTokenSource(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(1));
var gitHubService = host.Services.GetRequiredService<GitHubService>();
var gitHubZen = await gitHubService.GetZenAsync(cts.Token);
```

As explained [in the documentation](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly#optimistic-timeout), using an optimistic timeout requires you to use a delegate overload which receives a `CancellationToken` parameter, which you would then pass to the `HttpClient` instance. This would then cause the request to be cancelled after 30 milliseconds (or sooner if the value of `HttpClient.Timeout` is less than 30 milliseconds):

```csharp
 return await _policy.ExecuteAsync(
    async (cancellationToken) =>
        await _httpClientWrapper.GetAsync(httpRequest, cancellationToken).ConfigureAwait(false))
    .ConfigureAwait(false);
```

Otherwise, you need to use a [pessimistic timeout](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly#pessimistic-timeout), which will cause the code calling through the policy to observe an exception if it takes more than 30 milliseconds (at the expense of an additional task/thread to observe the HTTP request to time it does not take too long).

```csharp
public YourConstructor()
{
    _policy = Policy.TimeoutAsync(TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(30), , TimeoutStrategy.Pessimistic);
}
```

Optimistic retries are the recommended approach with `HttpClient`, so you just need to pass through the `CancellationToken`, which would cause the request to timeout as you require.

