Constructors are typically supposed to complete quickly and should not typically block on async work. If you move the async work out of the constructor, as below:

    public class MainApp
    {
        private IHttpClientFactory httpClientFactory;

        public MainApp(IHttpClientFactory httpClientFactory) { this.httpClientFactory = httpClientFactory; }

        public async Task DoWork()
        {
            //Creation HttpClient
            HttpClient httpClient = httpClientFactory.CreateClient("EiHttpClient");
            string url = "https://httpstat.us/401";

            //Launch HttpRequest
            var challengeHttpResponse = await httpClient.GetAsync(new Uri(url));

            Console.WriteLine($"got status code {challengeHttpResponse.StatusCode}");
        }
    }

then you can declare both services as singletons and there is no deadlock:

    services.AddSingleton<MainApp>();
    services.AddSingleton<IEIAuthentication, EIAuthenticationService>();

Attached is a complete ZIP demonstrating that this works: [Issue751_PollyAndDI-fixed.zip](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/files/4135574/Issue751_PollyAndDI-fixed.zip)

---

If you have a class to initialize which does depend on loading some async operation to be ready for use, consider the **Asynchronous Initialization Pattern**: move the async work out of the constructor, as [discussed by Stephen Cleary here](https://blog.stephencleary.com/2013/01/async-oop-2-constructors.html).

