Hi @bramakrishna16 .  Yes, we can configure a policy using `AddPolicyHandler()` that will act on the particular `HttpRequestMessage` of each request.

Polly offers an execution-scoped [`Polly.Context`](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/Keys-And-Context-Data) class:

+ An individual instance of this class travels with each execution through Polly.  Where policies are configured on an `HttpClient` using HttpClientFactory, a single instance travels with each call through `HttpClient`.
+ That `Polly.Context` [has dictionary semantics](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/Keys-And-Context-Data#using-context-for-custom-data) - you can store any custom data on it.
+ That `Polly.Context` instance is passed to all delegates you can configure on policies, [including the `onRetryAsync`](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/blob/4db50346299f62c2373ceba99d2522a8171c9e27/src/Polly/Retry/AsyncRetryEngine.cs#L76) - so you can use it to pass execution-specific data into the `onRetryAsync`, to avoid those [problematic closures](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/issues/689#issuecomment-535226843).

So for your case, the strategy will be to pass the execution-specific `HttpRequestMessage` to the `onRetryAsync` as a dictionary value on `Context`.

See here for documentation of the general approach with HttpClientFactory: [Exchanging information between policy execution and calling code](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/Polly-and-HttpClientFactory#use-case-exchanging-information-between-policy-execution-and-calling-code).

---

Taking your case in detail:

The `onRetryAsync` in the new `SelectNonRootLevelRetryPolicy` could change to something like: 

```csharp
    onRetryAsync: async (response, delay, retryCount, context) =>
    {
        if (context.TryGetValue("HttpRequestMessageKey", out var messageObject) && messageObject is HttpRequestMessage requestMessage)
        {
            // Rest of this onRetryAsync exactly as before, but acts on the requestMessage passed in via Polly.Context
            if (requestMessage.Headers.Contains("RequestAttemptCountHeader")) //checks for duplicate key insertion
            {
                requestMessage.Headers.Remove("RequestAttemptCountHeader");
            }

            requestMessage.Headers.Add("RequestAttemptCountHeader", retryCount.ToString());
            string destinationService = (requestMessage.Headers.Contains("DestinationService") ? requestMessage.Headers.GetValues("DestinationService").FirstOrDefault() : "Unknown");
            await LogRetry(sp, response, delay, retryCount, settings.RetriableErrors, settings.NonRootLevelRetryCount, destinationService, logger);
        }
    }
```

Where you execute through the policy, you create the `HttpRequestMessage` and store it on the `Polly.Context` before executing:

```csharp
HttpRequestMessage request = new HttpRequestMessage(HttpMethod.Get, requestUri); // ... for example - or however you need to create your request
var context = new Polly.Context();
context["HttpRequestMessageKey"] = request; // This stores the HttpRequestMessage that the onRetryAsync will pull out (if invoked) during execution.

request.SetPolicyExecutionContext(context);
var response = await client.SendAsync(request);
```

With this technique, you have to use one of the `HttpClient.SendAsync(...)` overloads which takes an `HttpRequestMessage` parameter (as shown above). But you can wrap the code snippet immediately above in a helper extension method on `HttpClient`, to make it more concise to consume.

Thanks @CreepyGnome for all the extra info - it's a great use case for hearing how people are pushing the envelope with Polly.

Yes: constructed Polly policies [fulfil interfaces](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/issues/685#issuecomment-529181145), but the policy builder syntax is static or concrete-builder-class methods, and so not amenable to mocking as a strategy for testing.

I'll post thoughts separately around options for converting Polly's builder syntax to an interface/instance-method approach (longer topic), but **TL;DR** it wouldn't happen overnight; so this concentrates on immediate practical suggestions for testing the dynamic policy generation code you have.

---

Generating policies dynamically at runtime need not be incompatible with a DI-based approach (notwithstanding aspects to your code I may be unaware). You could consider pulling a _policy factory_ out of the code described, something like:

```csharp
interface IPolicyFactory {
    IAsyncPolicy CreatePolicy();
}
```

(_I'm not sure from the code described whether your repositories are non-generic_ `SomeRepository` _or generic_ `SomeRepository<T>` _or indeed, either way, whether the policies used are non-generic_ `IAsyncPolicy` _or generic_ `IAsyncPolicy<T>`. I'll go with non-generic; adapt to `IAsyncPolicy<T>` if needed.)

The ctor for `SomeRepository` would then become something like:

```csharp
public SomeRepository(IPolicyFactory policyFactory, ILogger<SomeRepository> logger, IMongoClient client) 
```

with some concrete implementation `RepositoryPolicyFactory : IPolicyFactory` taking the `IOptionsSnapshot<SomeRepoSettings> settings` in its ctor:

```csharp
public RepositoryPolicyFactory(IOptionsSnapshot<SomeRepoSettings> settings, /* any any other params needed */)
```

ie the `IPolicyFactory` becomes responsible for/encapsulates manufacturing policies, while the `SomeRepository` (or `MongoDbRetrier`, depending on your exact code) is responsible for executing through the policy.

You could then isolate Polly out of repository tests where needed, by injecting a `NoOpPolicyFactory` into `SomeRepository` for those tests, where:

```csharp
public class NoOpPolicyFactory : IPolicyFactory
{
    IAsyncPolicy CreatePolicy() => Policy.NoOpAsync();
}
```

You could also construct a `MockPolicyFactory`:

```csharp
public class MockPolicyFactory : IPolicyFactory
{
    private Mock<IAsyncPolicy> mockPolicy;
    public MockPolicyfactory(Mock<IAsyncPolicy> mockPolicy) => this.mockPolicy = mockPolicy;
    IAsyncPolicy CreatePolicy() => mockPolicy.Object;
}
```

A test can then pass a `MockPolicyFactory` instance into `SomeRepository` and assert that `SomeRepository` (or its internal MongoRetrier) does execute through the policy the `IPolicyFactory` supplies.

This is essentially [suggestion 1 from our unit-testing wiki page](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/Unit-testing-with-Polly#1-i-want-to-unit-test-what-my-code-does-independent-of-polly-policies-i-apply) but applied with policy factories - since you generate policies dynamically.

Hope this is useful - the aim is to show concretely you can still take a DI approach to isolating concerns when generating policies dynamically.

The preceding suggestions still leave the problem of how to test the dynamic policy generation (ie your _real_ implementation `RepositoryPolicyFactory : IPolicyFactory` or similar), absent mockability.

I'm assuming the emphasis on mockability was in order to test _how_ policies were generated or used, using `mock.Verify(...)` or similar (given the other main use cases for mocking - stubbing out expensive objects or downstream systems - don't apply to policies). Say if I've inferred wrongly.

Absent mockability of PolicyBuilder, we have to test what has been generated, not how it is generated.

That a retry policy handles a given exception N number of times could be tested with a helper method like this:

```csharp
public static bool HandlesN(this RetryPolicy retryPolicy, int n, Exception ex)
{
    var catch = Policy<bool>.Handle<Exception>().Fallback(false);
    int i = 0;
    return catch.Wrap(retryPolicy).Execute(() => i++ < n ? throw ex : true);
}
```

(_EDIT: forgot the case in question may in async; if you need an async example, let me know_)

So if the responsibility of your `RepositoryPolicyFactory : IPolicyFactory` is to generate retry policies from config, which will handle given exceptions given numbers of times, that could be used to test it does.

```csharp
