Based on earlier comments and your [question here](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/pull/16#issuecomment-5284871620), I'll address the case that you have, or may have in future, code to dynamically create a `PolicyBuilder` to handle exceptions of a given `Type` instance, like [requested here](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/pull/16#issuecomment-528487162).

That a given concrete `PolicyBuilder` instance handles a given exception could be tested with a helper method like this:

```csharp
public static bool Handles(this PolicyBuilder builder, Exception ex)
{
    var catchEverythingElse = Policy<bool>.Handle<Exception>().Fallback(false);
    var builderTester = builder.Retry(1);
    int i = 0;
    return catchEverythingElse.Wrap(builderTester).Execute(() => i++ == 0 ? throw ex : true);
}
// or just
public static bool Handles(this PolicyBuilder builder, Exception ex) => builder.Retry(1).HandlesN(1, ex);
```

Using [this code](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/pull/16#issuecomment-529181189) just because it is a concrete example to refer to (_you could easily make that code instance methods if you prefer_), eg:

```csharp
var handleClause = PolicyExtensions.Handle(typeof(MongoClientException));
Assert.IsTrue(handleClause.Handles(new MongoClientException());
Assert.IsFalse(handleClause.Handles(new SqlException());
```

If you were to take the ^^ previously-suggested approach of factoring out an `IPolicyFactory`, you could split it further, pulling an `IPolicyBuilderFactory` out of it for the parts that build the handle-clauses of the policies:

```csharp
interface IPolicyBuilderFactory {
    PolicyBuilder CreatePolicyBuilder();
}
```

The concrete implementations of `IPolicyBuilderFactory` could then be tested with the `public static bool Handles(this PolicyBuilder builder, Exception ex)` extension method. 

Hope useful - aim is to demonstrate some concrete helpers for testing the dynamic policy generation discussed.

