It is absolutely safe from Polly's perspective to elide:

    .ExecuteAsync(async () =>{ return await code().ConfigureAwait(false); });

to:

    .ExecuteAsync(code);

All Polly policy implementations `await` internally the `Func<Task<..., ..., T>>` you pass to `.ExecuteAsync(...)`. And all Polly implementations use `.ConfigureAwait(false)` internally on all `await`s, unless you execute with `continueOnCapturedContext: true`.

---

Eliding:

    async Task<T> Retry<T>(Func<Task<T>> code) => { return await GetRetryPolicy().ExecuteAsync(code); }

to:

    Task<T> Retry<T>(Func<Task<T>> code) => { return GetRetryPolicy().ExecuteAsync(code); }

can cause exceptions thrown by `GetRetryPolicy()` to express differently as I'm sure you will have seen in Stephen Cleary's article.  Whether that is a concern is independent of Polly, and depends on how you are calling your wrapper method `Retry<T>(...)`.

