@maklipsa . Here is a _sketch of a concept_ to get you a quick answer (rather than leave you waiting longer for an answer).  The behaviour can be expressed with existing Polly features using a pattern such as below.  _Note:_ this is a sketch to give you all the elements of a solution; you will likely want to factor parts of this out into helper classes or methods, add defences for edge-cases, proper cancellation etc.

*TL;DR*: for separate circuit-breaking per downstream server, create, maintain and reuse an instance of a circuit-breaker per downstream server.

---

Given:

    string[] servers = new []
    {
        "http://server1.whatever.com",
        "http://server2.whatever.com",
        "http://server3.whatever.com",
    };

    const string ServerIndexKey = "ServerIndexKey";

    PolicyRegistry registry = new PolicyRegistry();

Declare policies such as:

    Func<IAsyncPolicy<string>> circuitBreakerFactory = () => Policy<string>.Handle<Exception>().CircuitBreakerAsync(3, TimeSpan.FromSeconds(10)); // Or more refined fault-handling, such as only certain exceptions; also http result-codes

    var multipleServerRetryPolicy = Policy
        .Handle<Exception>() // Or more refined fault-filtering ...
        .WaitAndRetryForeverAsync(
            sleepDurationProvider: (attempt, context) => TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(30)
            , onRetry: (exception, timespan, context) => // Of course, can factor this out to a nicely-named method SelectAnotherServer() or similar.
            {
                int currentServer = context.GetValueOrDefault<int>(ServerIndexKey);
                currentServer = (currentServer + 1) % servers.Length;
                context[ServerIndexKey] = currentServer;
            }
        );

And execute with:

    var text = await multipleServerRetryPolicy.ExecuteAsync(async (context, token) =>
            {
                int server = context.GetValueOrDefault<int>(ServerIndexKey);
                string url = $"{servers[server]}/localpath/etc"; // Or of course, the localpath part passed in from outside ...
                var breaker = registry.GetOrAdd($"breaker{server}", circuitBreakerFactory);
                return await breaker.ExecuteAsync(() => client.GetStringAsync(url));
            }, new Context(), default(CancellationToken));

( **EDIT**: If you wrap this into a helper class which maintains state of the last-used server, then you can also prevent the next call starting with server 0 (zero) again, by passing `new Context(){[ServerIndexKey] = lastServer}` in to `Executeasync(....)` rather than just `new Context()`. )

Where you have extension methods:

    public static IAsyncPolicy<TResult> GetOrAdd<TResult>(this PolicyRegistry registry, string key, Func<IAsyncPolicy<TResult>> policyFactory)
    {
        if (registry.TryGet(key, out IAsyncPolicy<TResult> policy))
        {
            return policy;
        }

        var newPolicy = policyFactory();
        registry[key] = newPolicy;
        return newPolicy;
    }

    public static T GetValueOrDefault<T>(this Context context, string key)
    {
        if (context.TryGetValue(key, out object objValue))
        {
            return objValue is T value ? value : default;
        }
        return default;
    }

---

Explanations (in relation to the solution above, and some points raised in your question):

+ Circuit-breaker state is not scoped to `OperationKey`.
+ Circuit-breaker state is intentionally scoped to circuit-breaker policy instance.  So, for separate circuit-breaking per downstream server, create, maintain and reuse an instance of a circuit-breaker per downstream server.  The above code uses [`PolicyRegistry`](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/PolicyRegistry) to do this. Of course, you could use other techniques like just manage an array `IAsyncPolicy<string>[]` 
+ The retry policies do have `onRetry:` overloads taking `Context` as an input parameter - shown above.

---

Let us know if that is enough info; or if any questions.

