@rmandvikar Thanks for the [great example](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/issues/477#issuecomment-402945772).

What is happening is that the `fallback` policy you have configured is replacing the `TimeoutRejectedException` with a replacement result of `null` (`default(object)`). This is [bubbled (as the overall result) back out to `.ExecuteAndCaptureAsync(....)`, which says](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/blob/f4404005b5c1e4965b74b3fe01a6b4ec0642f97a/src/Polly.Shared/Policy.Async.TResult.ExecuteOverloads.cs#L262-L269) "`null` isn't something the `FallbackPolicy` regards as a fault, so now we have a successful outcome!"   `.ExecuteAndCaptureAsync(....)` is capturing and evaluating the final outcome, after the substitution by fallback.

I would agree if you thought this behaviour of `.ExecuteAndCapture/Async(...)` with `FallbackPolicy` is subtle! But it makes sense: in your original fallback policy, you replace the failure outcome with a success outcome ... so the overall outcome should be reported as success!

If you replace the `FallbackPolicy` with one which doesn't substitute a different result, but bubbles the received result back outwards, then indeed the overall execution outcome is a fault, and the test will pass:

```csharp

        [Fact]
        public async Task Outcome_Should_Be_Failure()
        {
            var retryCount = 1000;
            var retry = Policy
                .Handle<Exception>()
                .OrResult<object>((result) => false)
                .WaitAndRetryAsync(
                    retryCount,
                    ra => TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(1000));
            var timeout = Policy
                .TimeoutAsync(1);
            var fallback = Policy
                .Handle<TimeoutRejectedException>()
                .OrResult<object>((result) => false)
                .FallbackAsync(
                    fallbackAction: (outcome, ctx, ct) => {
                        if (outcome.Exception != null) throw outcome.Exception; // bubbles a received exception back outwards // or use ExceptionDispatchInfo to preserve stack trace
                        return Task.FromResult(outcome.Result); // bubbles a handled result back outwards
                    },
                    onFallbackAsync: (outcome, ctx) => Task.CompletedTask
                );
            var policy = fallback.WrapAsync(timeout).WrapAsync(retry);

            var time = DateTime.UtcNow;
            var retryAttempt = -1;

            var policyResult = await policy.ExecuteAndCaptureAsync(async (ct) =>
                {
                    ct.ThrowIfCancellationRequested();
                    retryAttempt++;
                    throw new Exception();
                    return await Task.FromResult(new object());
                }, System.Threading.CancellationToken.None)
                .ConfigureAwait(false);

            var actualTimeout = DateTime.UtcNow.Subtract(time).TotalSeconds;
            Assert.IsTrue(retryAttempt < retryCount);
            Assert.AreEqual(OutcomeType.Failure, policyResult.Outcome);
            Assert.IsNull(policyResult.Result);
        }
```

