Hi @joshidp . `PolicyWrap` is designed always to contain two (or by nesting, more) policies.

If you want sometimes to apply only a single policy, sometimes multiple policies, based on a supplied `Policy[] policies`, you could use a helper method:

```
public Policy WrapOrSingleAsync(Policy[] policies)
{
    switch(policies.Length)
    {
         case 0: 
            throw new ArgumentException(/* some error message that no policies were supplied */);
         case 1: 
            return policies[0];
         default: 
            return Policy.WrapAsync(policies);
    }
}
```

The subsequent code to execute delegates using the returned `Policy` will be identical regardless of whether the returned value is a single policy or wrap, since [PolicyWrap is just another kind of Policy](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/PolicyWrap#policywrap-is-a-policy).

---

The constraint that a `PolicyWrap` always contains >=2 policies reduces run-time checks on `PolicyWrap`, but does require a workround such as above in the case you want a 'wrap containing only one Policy'.

There would be trade-offs to changing this, but I'll revisit the possibilities as part of a future release.

@joshidp Polly provides a `PolicyRegistry` (see [readme](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly#policyregistry); [wiki](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/PolicyRegistry)).  

A common pattern using Polly with IoC is to configure policies and place them in the `PolicyRegistry` on startup, and then register the `PolicyRegistry` on the IoC container.  At point of use, retrieve the `PolicyRegistry` isntance by DI/IoC and extract the policy you need from it.

You can use Polly's out-of-box `PolicyRegistry`, or [fulfil the `IPolicyRegistry<TKey>` interface](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/PolicyRegistry#implementing-an-alternative-registry-with-ipolicyregistryin-tkey) to design more complex registries if needed.

