Not a problem, just want to make sure our advice is focused on what you need.

Re:
>>Are you looking to separate policy definition and usage? (ie you define the policy outside this method and pass it in as an IRetryPolicy or IPolicyAsync).

>Not necessarily - but if this is the only way to get rid of the dependency on the Policy class, then yes.

Injecting policies to make the code more testable does imply separating policy definition and usage.  Rather than declaring the policy within the class/method where it is used (which means you can't change it for testing), you declare it outside, so that it (the dependency) can be injected.  By having it injected, you can then substitute something else in a test.

And because you've separated policy definition from usage, you can't close over the `_sqlConnection` variable any more, so you have to pass it through Polly's execution `Context`

You don't have to switch to interfaces to use DI - you can inject anything, either declared as a concrete `Policy myPolicy` class or an `IPolicyAsync myPolicy` interface, but interfaces is typical (advantages a longer discussion not for here).  

A sketch using the code you provided, and Polly interfaces:

```
// Somewhere outside the method or class you define the policy.  Often at app startup.
// This policy definition shows extracting the _sqlConnection instance from the Context.  At the call site, you'll pass it in.
// You don't need the retryCount variable in your original; Polly provides that as the passed in int retryAttempt

IAsyncPolicy myPolicy = Policy
		.Handle<SqlException>()
		.WaitAndRetryAsync(3, retryAttempt => TimeSpan.FromMilliseconds(0.1 * retryAttempt),
			(ex, timeSpan, context) => {
				 ((ConnectionClass)context["sqlConnection"])?.Close();
			})


// Where you're actually using the policy - typically, you might inject it through a class constructor and store it in a private field:
// (but there can be other approaches)

    class MyClassUsingThePolicy
    {
        private IAsyncPolicy _myInjectedPolicy;
        MyClassUsingThePolicy(IAsyncPolicy myInjectedPolicy)
        {
            _myInjectedPolicy = myInjectedPolicy;
        }

        public async void MyMethodUsingThePolicy()
        {
            // Assumption: ConnectionClass _sqlConnection is declared and assigned somewhere ... use also the c

            try
            {
                await _myInjectedPolicy
                    .ExecuteAsync(async context =>
                    {
                        var sqlConnection = ((ConnectionClass)context["sqlConnection"]);
                        await sqlConnection.OpenAsync();
                        await sqlConnection.Query<Test>("SELECT * FROM Test"); 
                    },
                    new Dictionary<string, object>() { { "sqlConnection", _sqlConnection } } // This uses the `.ExecuteAsync(...)` overload that takes dictionary data that will be placed on the `Context`
                    );
            }
            finally
            {
                _sqlConnection.Close();
            }

        }
```

(A few details weren't provided in your original code sample, like the type of `_sqlConnection` .... maybe it's using a micro ORM like NPoco or somethig, due to the `.Query<Test>` syntax? ,.. adjust the type as necessary)

For unit tests, you can then pass in a `IAsyncPolicy myStubPolicy = Policy.NoOpAsync()` to the constructor of `MyClassUsingThePolicy`, instead of the real policy, to stub out the effects of Polly from the test.

