(I've intentionally elided many of the guard conditions against `null` for brevity and to focus on the structure; you probably want to keep them.)

The exception conditions actually handled by the `catch` in the original code can be condensed into one line, as a handle condition for a Polly policy:

`Policy.Handle<WebException>(wex => wex.Response?.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.InternalServerError)`

The original code substitutes one thing for another, on error, in two possible ways: one `HttpWebResponse` for another; and ultimately one `string` return value for another.  The Polly policy for providing a substitute response when something fails is [`FallbackPolicy`](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/Fallback).  

`FallbackPolicy` is strongly-typed to the type of result you want to substitute, for [compile-time type-binding with the type of delegates you execute through it](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/Non-generic-and-generic-policies#binding-handleresulttresult-and-executetresult).  So we need a separate `FallbackPolicy` for each of the `HttpWebResponse` and `string` responses.  But we can write the code to express the handled conditions only once:

```c#
var handleConditions = Policy.Handle<WebException>(wex => wex.Response?.StatusCode == HttpStatusCode.InternalServerError);

// NOTE: This overload of FallbackPolicy will not be available until enhancement #278 is delivered.
FallbackPolicy responseFallback = handleConditions 
    .Fallback<HttpWebResponse>((failure, context, cancellationToken) => failure.Exception.Response); // equivalent to mapping to wex.Response in your original catch (WebException wex)

// NOTE: This overload of FallbackPolicy will not be available until enhancement #278 is delivered.
FallbackPolicy bodyFallback = handleConditions
    .Fallback<string>((failure, context, cancellationToken) => { 
         using (HttpWebResponse response = (HttpWebResponse) failure.Exception.Response)
         { 
             return GetStreamAsStringFromResponse(response));
         } 
     });
```

The overall execution then condenses to just a few lines:

```c#
private string Query(string address) {
    HttpWebRequest webRequest = (HttpWebRequest) WebRequest.Create(new Uri(address));
    using (HttpWebResponse response = responseFallback.Execute(() => webRequest.GetResponse()))  
    {
        return bodyFallback.Execute(() => GetStreamAsStringFromResponse(response));
    }

```

