Thanks @TylerNielsen for the question.

>Would there be any way to let the execution finish but simply not start another retry if the timeout had expired?

Yes. Use `TimeoutStrategy.Optimistic` (which operates [only by co-operative cancellation](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/Timeout#optimistic-timeout)) and have your execution simply not honour the `CancellationToken`.  It will then not be interrupted by TimeoutPolicy.  But the retry engine (which [does honour the cancellation token](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/blob/4db50346299f62c2373ceba99d2522a8171c9e27/src/Polly/Retry/RetryEngine.cs#L28)) will block any further retry if the timeout has elapsed when a new try is due.

For example:

    public static void Main(string[] args)
    {
        Stopwatch watch = Stopwatch.StartNew();
        var timeoutPolicy = Policy.Timeout(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(4.5)); // TimeoutStrategy.Optimistic by default
        var retryPolicy = Policy.Handle<Exception>().Retry(5, (e, i) => Console.WriteLine("Starting retry " + i + " after elapsed ms: " + watch.ElapsedMilliseconds));
        timeoutPolicy.Wrap(retryPolicy).Execute(() => LongOperation());
    }

    static void LongOperation()
    {
        Console.WriteLine("Starting long task");
        Console.WriteLine("Long work: Delaying 3 seconds");
        Thread.Sleep(TimeSpan.FromSeconds(3));
        Console.WriteLine("Completing long task");
        Console.WriteLine("Oops- no, it's throwing after all...");
        throw new Exception();
    }

([can be tried in .NET Fiddle here](https://dotnetfiddle.net/YN3wl3)), gives this output:

    Starting long task
    Long work: Delaying 3 seconds
    Completing long task
    Oops- no, it's throwing after all...
    Starting retry 1 after elapsed ms: 3033
    Starting long task
    Long work: Delaying 3 seconds
    Completing long task
    Oops- no, it's throwing after all...
    Starting retry 2 after elapsed ms: 6045
    Run-time exception (line 13): The delegate executed through TimeoutPolicy did not complete within the timeout.

Execution is not abandoned after 4.5 seconds; retry 1 is allowed to run to completion, retry 2 is only cued up after 6 seconds; TimeoutPolicy does not abandon execution until it gets a chance at the start of retry 2.  Importantly: if retry 1 had succeeded and was returning a result, the calling code would get that result, not a timeout.  

_EDIT:_ This is in fact the same solution I gave the OP.  In optimistic mode, the code executed not being interrupted by TimeoutPolicy simply depends on it ignoring the `CancellationToken`.  If it honours the `CancellationToken`, it can _choose_ to terminate earlier that way.

