### Possibilities with Polly

To get a combination of retry and circuit-breaker semantics with Polly, you can combine retry and circuit-breaker policies using [PolicyWrap](https://github.com/App-vNext/Polly/wiki/PolicyWrap), or manually.  

```csharp
TimeSpan coolOffPeriod = TimeSpan.FromMinutes(5);
TimeSpan acceptableLatencyOfSuccessfulCall = /* define this */ ; 
DateTimeOffset retryAfter = DateTimeOffset.MinValue;

var breaker = Policy
    .Handle<ExceptionThrownByUnderlyingSystem>() 
    .CircuitBreakerAsync(
        1, // Block other callers as soon as a single occurrence of the governed exception occurs.
        coolOffPeriod, // Nobody can place a call in the cool-off period - and only one thread can retry, when the cool-off period is complete.
        (ex, breakDuration) => { retryAfter = DateTimeOffset.UtcNow.Add(breakDuration); }
    );
var headOfQueueRetry = Policy
    .Handle<ExceptionThrownByUnderlyingSystem>() // First call which causes circuit to break will still throw this.
    .WaitAndRetryAsync(1, i => coolOffPeriod) // Retry only once, as soon as cool-off period has elapsed.  
    );
var behindHeadOfQueueRetry = Policy
    .Handle<BrokenCircuitException>()  // Subsequent calls hitting broken circuit will throw this.
    .WaitAndRetryAsync( 
        1, // Only retry once.
        i => {   
                 TimeSpan remainingWait = (retryAfter - DateTimeOffset.UtcNow) // Programatically determine how long a call arriving during the cool-off period, should wait, so it's retried at the end of the cool-off period. 
                     + acceptableLatencyOfSuccessfulCall;  // But the 'waiting' threads want to give the thread at the head of the queue, enough time for its trial execution to complete successfully, before the waiting threads retry.
                 return remainingWait <= TimeSpan.Zero ? TimeSpan.Zero : remainingWait;
             }
    );
var combinedStrategy = behindHeadOfQueueRetry.WrapAsync(headOfQueueRetry.WrapAsync(breaker));

await combinedStrategy.ExecuteAsync(ct => { /* etc */ }, cancellationToken);

```

Not necessarily a finished solution, but illustrates possibilities.    

