@penderi  Regarding combining policies, yes, this is the way we have done it too.  For example, in a microservices environment publishing messages to a message broker like RabbitMQ, we are nesting retry and circuit-breaker with something like:

``` csharp
public Task TransientFaultProtectedPublishAsync(Func<IMessage, Task> publishAction, IMessage message)
{
    var retryPolicy = ... 
    var circuitBreakerPolicy = ...
    Task messagePublishTask = retryPolicy.ExecuteAsync(() =>
        {
            circuitBreakerPolicy.ExecuteAsync(() =>
            {
                publishAction.Invoke(message);
            });
        });
    return messagePublishTask;
}
```

(In reality it's decorated with a bunch of `.ContinueWith(...)` for error-tracking, and our policy instances are initialised/defined elsewhere, but this gives a clearer idea of structure)

